WEBVTT

00:00:13.916 --> 00:00:15.250
-I'm gonna jump.
-What?

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:16.291
No, no. What are you...

00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:18.875
Carrots, hey... I would like
to call a partner meeting.

00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:20.041
No, Carrots!

00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:22.083
-Judy!
-[Judy yelps]

00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:31.083
[♪ music stops]

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:33.166
Please join me
in a moment of silence

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.333
for our beloved weather wall
inventor Ebenezer Lynxley.

00:00:37.416 --> 00:00:38.416
[gasps]

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.500
-[laughs]
-[both] Zebros!

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.625
[sighs]

00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:47.208
-[hinge squeaks]
-Huh?

00:00:52.041 --> 00:00:53.083
[gasps]

00:00:58.458 --> 00:00:59.875
Reptile?

00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:02.333
[gasps]

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.875
Chief, those two
are out of control!

00:01:05.958 --> 00:01:08.916
Zootropolis ain't just
a mammal city.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.458
It has a secret,
tiny reptile population,

00:01:13.541 --> 00:01:15.333
though most live abroad.

00:01:15.416 --> 00:01:19.250
Of course, Zootropolis does not
allow snakes.
[chuckles]

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:21.041
There ain't been one of those
around here

00:01:21.125 --> 00:01:23.000
for at least a hundred years.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.458
[announcer]
"Scales and tales
of the weird."

00:01:24.541 --> 00:01:26.000
NIBBLES MAPLESTICK
SCALES & TALES OF THE WEIRD

00:01:26.083 --> 00:01:29.125
Yeah, not sure
Nibbles Maplestick

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:31.625
is our most reliable source.

00:01:31.708 --> 00:01:34.791
[Chief Bogo] Bunny,
orange dog, in here now.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:35.916
[grumbles]

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.041
[Judy] Sir, today may not
have been ideal,

00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:38.958
but the anteater was captured.

00:01:39.041 --> 00:01:40.291
By the Zebros!

00:01:40.375 --> 00:01:41.875
-Zebros!
-Shut it!

00:01:42.541 --> 00:01:43.791
[both whisper] Zebros.

00:01:43.875 --> 00:01:44.875
It was us.

00:01:44.958 --> 00:01:46.125
But more importantly,

00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:49.125
I believe we may have made
a significant discovery.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:52.333
The stolen van contained a
smuggled crate from overseas,

00:01:52.416 --> 00:01:53.708
pamphlets for the Zootennial,

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:55.625
and some type of reptile skin.

00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:57.291
I've already sent samples
for testing.

00:01:57.375 --> 00:01:59.958
-And if you...
-What needs testing is you!

00:02:00.041 --> 00:02:02.375
You two tore up half the city.

00:02:02.458 --> 00:02:05.000
Because of you,
we had to call in Jumbo Unit

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.083
to remove a dik-dik
from a tuba!

00:02:07.166 --> 00:02:08.583
You're almost out.
You're gonna be...

00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:10.708
No! Please, no!

00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:12.416
Who's catching him?

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.125
Sorry, could you show me
that clip again?

00:02:14.208 --> 00:02:15.500
Wasn't wearing my glasses.

00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:20.083
Did you or did you not disobey
a direct order to stand down?

00:02:20.166 --> 00:02:21.666
Sir, we were in pursuit.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.500
And article 6,
paragraph B states,

00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:24.875
"If the lead officers
think that..."

00:02:24.958 --> 00:02:26.625
You are not lead officers!

00:02:26.708 --> 00:02:28.000
You're one-hit wonders

00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:29.500
who should go back
to meter maiding

00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:31.416
and slinging pawpsicles!

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.125
If I may, I think
someone's just jealous

00:02:34.208 --> 00:02:35.875
that we got to drive
the squeal mobile.

00:02:35.958 --> 00:02:38.500
Or maybe you thought
it was your momma.

00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:41.583
-[squealing, growling]
-Enough! Everybody out.

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:43.375
[grunting]

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.375
[sighs]

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:48.125
Officer Hopps,

00:02:48.208 --> 00:02:52.208
despite my best efforts
to avoid it, I like you.

00:02:52.791 --> 00:02:57.666
But this need to overdo it

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.583
made both of you
a headline today.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.791
And it reflects badly on me,
on the department,

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:06.333
and frankly

00:03:06.416 --> 00:03:10.583
on any bunny hoping
to follow in your footsteps.

00:03:12.541 --> 00:03:15.333
Not every case
is going to save the world.

00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:18.083
Knock-knock.

00:03:18.583 --> 00:03:19.583
Hi.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:21.166
You know, this kinda...
this sounds a lot

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.291
like a... just a
"you guys" conversation.

00:03:23.375 --> 00:03:25.250
So what I'm gonna do
is I'm gonna go ahead and...

00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:28.291
Is there a reason why you
don't take anything seriously?

00:03:28.375 --> 00:03:29.708
Jokes are
a classic defense mechanism

00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:31.125
for someone with
a traumatic childhood.

00:03:31.208 --> 00:03:33.125
Would you like
a traumatic adulthood?

00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:35.125
I would not.

00:03:35.208 --> 00:03:36.541
I allowed you to work together

00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:39.666
because you did this city
a great service.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.750
But today,
you messed it all up.

00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:44.833
And now, some are questioning

00:03:44.916 --> 00:03:46.750
whether you should've been
partners in the first place.

00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:47.958
-Sir...
-So,

00:03:48.041 --> 00:03:50.541
instead of chasing
imaginary reptiles,

00:03:50.625 --> 00:03:52.166
I am pulling you
from the field...

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.083
What?

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.916
...and giving you
a new assignment

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.500
reserved for special teams
like yourselves.

00:03:58.583 --> 00:04:03.541
Fail at this, and I will have
no choice but to split you up.

00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:06.291
There will be no more
Hopps and Wilde.

00:04:06.916 --> 00:04:10.916
But if you're as good
as you think you are,

00:04:11.708 --> 00:04:13.791
this is your moment to shine.

00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:19.500
Welcome to Partners in Crisis,

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:22.916
a workshop for duos heading
for disaster. [chuckles]

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.833
I'm your therapy animal,
Dr. Fuzzby.

00:04:25.916 --> 00:04:26.916
And you are here

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.708
because mismatched pairings
often need a little more work

00:04:30.791 --> 00:04:32.708
to make them work.

00:04:32.791 --> 00:04:35.166
Greg, we talked about that.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.458
What are we making Marlon?

00:04:36.541 --> 00:04:38.916
Uncomfortable. Yes.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:39.833
[screams]

00:04:39.916 --> 00:04:42.041
[Dr. Fuzzby] And, Francine,
Clark may be a mouse,

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:44.250
but he is your partner first.

00:04:45.416 --> 00:04:48.041
And we have two
new "apart-ners."

00:04:48.625 --> 00:04:50.750
Can you tell us
about your journey

00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:52.958
to dysfunction junction?
[chuckles]

00:04:53.041 --> 00:04:57.166
Well, we've been official
partners for one week.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.041
Happy anniversary.

00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:01.666
And we're not dysfunctional
at all, actually.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.916
Functioning fine.
Better than fine.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.916
And we did sort of
save the city,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.625
so us being here kinda seems
like a huge misunderstanding.

00:05:10.666 --> 00:05:12.250
Notice how she answered first,

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:13.833
did not allow her partner
to speak,

00:05:13.916 --> 00:05:15.208
seems to be
in a state of denial

00:05:15.291 --> 00:05:17.458
and taps her foot when
she's suppressing discomfort.

00:05:17.541 --> 00:05:18.500
[chuckles]

00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:20.583
And observe the source
of her discomfort

00:05:20.666 --> 00:05:22.583
represented by
the disconnected affectation

00:05:22.666 --> 00:05:24.666
of her emotionally
insecure partner.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:25.583
Oh!

00:05:25.666 --> 00:05:28.583
But with hard work, they'll be
on the same page by year two.

00:05:28.666 --> 00:05:29.500
-Huh?
-Oh.

00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:32.750
But for now, let's begin
by greeting our teammate.

00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:34.291
Who would like to go first?

00:05:34.375 --> 00:05:35.500
Thank you, Joel.

00:05:37.208 --> 00:05:38.791
-[grunts, gasping]
-[snarling]

00:05:38.875 --> 00:05:40.916
Okay, unfortunately,
Karen has chosen

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.250
to see Joel's smile
as a sign of aggression.

00:05:44.333 --> 00:05:47.083
Hackles down, Karen.
Karen, hackles down.

00:05:47.166 --> 00:05:49.291
Joel, the safe word
is "coconut."

00:05:49.375 --> 00:05:50.625
[Joel] Coconut!

00:05:52.666 --> 00:05:54.833
Okay, we have to set another
meeting with the Chief.

00:05:54.916 --> 00:05:56.625
Promise we'll be better.

00:05:56.708 --> 00:05:57.958
-[gasps]
-[Zebros chuckle]

00:05:58.041 --> 00:05:59.208
[whispering] Zebros!

00:05:59.291 --> 00:06:02.291
[chuckles] You talk back less.
Maybe not at all.

00:06:02.375 --> 00:06:05.875
Yeah. Or we go with my
playbook, and we just lay low.

00:06:05.958 --> 00:06:07.875
[Judy scoffs]
We cannot prove ourselves

00:06:07.958 --> 00:06:09.041
if we are stuck in that room.

00:06:09.125 --> 00:06:10.583
And if we complain
to Chief Beef,

00:06:10.666 --> 00:06:12.083
he'll just make us
stay longer.

00:06:12.166 --> 00:06:13.875
Play it smart.
Stay off the radar.

00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:15.791
And don't let it get to you.
Okay?

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.958
'Cause we are the dream team.

00:06:18.458 --> 00:06:19.458
[gasps]

00:06:20.916 --> 00:06:21.916
Sorry, Paul.

00:06:23.041 --> 00:06:24.750
[growling, yelling]

00:06:25.875 --> 00:06:28.125
Roll out the red carpet.
Here's the super cops.

00:06:28.208 --> 00:06:30.375
-[heckling, shouting]
-Hey, what does the fox say?

00:06:30.458 --> 00:06:31.458
You suck! [laughing]

00:06:31.541 --> 00:06:32.750
Easy, partner.

00:06:32.833 --> 00:06:34.208
Let me ask you something.

00:06:34.291 --> 00:06:35.750
Do you think
we're a good team?

00:06:35.833 --> 00:06:37.208
Of course.

00:06:37.291 --> 00:06:38.458
I just...

00:06:38.541 --> 00:06:40.250
I just wish I knew
what we had to do

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:42.166
to prove it to everyone else.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.250
Or maybe we don't have
to prove anything to anybody.

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:46.250
[knocking]

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:48.625
-[laughing continues]
-[Judy] Hmm.

00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:51.041
-We do. Forever.
-Oof!

00:06:51.125 --> 00:06:52.541
And the sooner
everyone sees

00:06:52.625 --> 00:06:54.541
a fox and a bunny can be
great partners, the better.

00:06:54.625 --> 00:06:56.416
Enjoy it. I got two.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.208
I'd rather chew off
my own arm.

00:06:58.291 --> 00:07:00.083
Happy anniversary!

00:07:01.583 --> 00:07:05.458
[♪ upbeat music playing]

00:07:07.875 --> 00:07:09.416
-[grunts, gasping] Coconut!
-[snarling]

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.500
[Joel] Coconut!

